    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A peasant's daughter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a> who testified that visions of saints had told her to help the Dauphin Charles, Joan reached his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinon">Chinon</a> in 1429, aged seventeen.</li><li>Within months the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-orleans">siege of Orléans</a> was lifted, the English army was routed at Patay and Charles was consecrated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>; the failures at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and La-Charité-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> then reduced the court's faith in her.</li><li>Captured at Compiègne in 1430 and handed to the English for a ransom, she was tried for heresy and burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in 1431; the verdict was nullified in 1456 and she was canonized in 1920.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Joan may never have heard herself called &quot;Jeanne d'Arc&quot;: the first written record of that name dates from 1455, 24 years after her death. In her letters she called herself Jeanne la Pucelle, &quot;Joan the Maiden&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

